; Tutorial_00_Overview.inix -- the first walkthrough of a Homer app.
;
; THE FORMAT, IN ONE PLACE.
;
; Each [step] is one passage of narration and one of screen reader speech.
; buildTutorials speaks them with two voices and joins them into an .mp3 per
; script, then one .mkv with a chapter for each. makeTutorials writes the same
; scripts into Tutorials.md and the podcast feed.
;
; Keys in a [step]:
;   Say=    what the narrator says. One idea, no padding, no transitions.
;   Key=    the keystroke being demonstrated, in Homer key-name form.
;   Hear=   what the screen reader answers. One line per utterance, written the
;           way a fresh reader at its middle verbosity would say it: name, role,
;           value, state, position. Repeat the key for several utterances.
;   Note=   written only, never spoken. The aside a reader wants and a listener
;           does not.
;   Pause=  seconds of silence before the passage. SSML calls this
;           <break time="2s"/>; this is the same idea without the brackets.
;
; Keys in [about]: Title (numbered, "00 - Overview"), Intro, Setup, Homework.
; Keys in [feed], on the FIRST script only: Title, Description, Author, Email.
;
; FOUR BEATS PER KEY, the way a screen reader trainer narrates: Say the key
; (with its phonetic word: "Insert T, Tango"), press it, Hear the reader word
; for word, then a second Say that translates what was heard. Name silence
; ("(nothing)") when the reader says nothing; verify with Insert T after every
; change of focus; teach Insert Up Arrow (repeat) in the first script. The
; full list is in help\Tutorials.md, "How a screen reader trainer narrates".
;
; ALWAYS SAY THE WORD THE KEY COMES FROM. "Shift plus C is Say Cell -- C for
; Cell." A key whose mnemonic goes unsaid is a key somebody has to memorise
; rather than understand, and the memory association is the whole reason the
; key was chosen.

[feed]
Title=_APP_ Walkthroughs
Description=Short simulated walks through _APP_. Each one is about three minutes, with something to try at the end.
Author=Your Name
Email=you@example.com
Base=

[about]
Title=00 - Overview and Table of Contents
Intro=A simulated walk through _APP_, made with AI: a person working, and a screen reader answering.
Setup=Nothing yet. This one is two minutes of what to expect.
Homework=Pick the one that matches what you want to do today.

[step]
Say=What this program is, in two sentences.
Key=
Hear=
Note=Say what it does and who it is for, and stop.

[step]
Say=What each walkthrough covers, in the order they are numbered.
Key=
Hear=
Note=Name the two that have to be taken in order, and say the rest can be taken as needed.

[step]
Say=Every key is named with the word it comes from, so the key and the command stay together in memory.
Key=
Hear=
Note=Control plus F1 is the key describer in every Homer app: press it, then a key, and it says what the key does instead of doing it.
